{"etape": 13, "horodatage_utc": "2026-10-21T11:25:50.015439Z", "outil": "send_message_to_user", "arguments": {"message": "I've found the cheapest business-class round trip on your dates. Here's what it comes to:\n\n**Cheapest business itinerary**\n- **Outbound:** HAT023 JFK → SFO on 2024-05-26 — business **$364**/passenger (direct)\n- **Return:** HAT204 SFO → SEA (08:00–10:00) + HAT100 SEA → JFK (13:00–19:00) on 2024-05-28 — business **$248 + $259 = $507**/passenger\n- **Total per passenger: $871** → **3 passengers = $2,613**\n\n**One important limitation on payments:** our policy allows **at most one travel certificate per reservation**. So even though you have three certificates ($1,000 total), I can apply only one — I'd use the largest, the **$500** certificate, to minimize your card charge. The remaining certificates can't be combined on a single booking.\n\n**Payment application (maximizing non-card methods):**\n- Certificate (one): $500\n- Gift cards (both): $198 + $129 = $327\n- **Combined: $827**\n- **Mastercard remainder: $2,613 − $827 = $1,786**\n\nSince **$1,786 is not less than the $567** you were originally charged, your stated condition is **not met**, so this booking wouldn't go ahead under your rule.\n\n(Note: even if you waited for the $567 refund from cancelling to land back on your gift card — 5–7 business days — the Mastercard charge would still be about $1,219, still above $567.)\n\nSo it looks like the new business-class round trip is significantly more than your original charge. Would you like to explore business class on a different date, keep your current reservation, or something else?"}, "resultat": "Thank you for breaking down the costs and payment application. Since the Mastercard charge for the new flight would be higher than what I originally paid, I will not proceed with this new booking.\n\nI'd like to keep my current reservation as is. Thank you for your help!\n###STOP###", "erreur": null}
